{
  "segments": [
    {
      "id": 40,
      "text": "Thought experiment in distributed computing. Okay, break it down for us. So, imagine a massive army divided"
    },
    {
      "id": 41,
      "text": "into multiple battalions, and they're surrounding an enemy city. Okay, got it. The generals of these battalions need to agree on"
    },
    {
      "id": 42,
      "text": "a specific time to attack. But here's the catch: they don't have a central commander. So, no one person calling the shots."
    },
    {
      "id": 43,
      "text": "Right. They have to coordinate their plan by sending messengers back and forth across enemy lines, which sounds"
    },
    {
      "id": 44,
      "text": "like an incredibly unreliable network. It is. And to make it worse, they know that some of the generals are"
    },
    {
      "id": 45,
      "text": "actually traitors. Oh, wow. Traitors who will intentionally send fake messages to confuse the loyalists."
    },
    {
      "id": 46,
      "text": "So, the question is, how did the loyal generals reach an undeniable consensus on when to attack despite the bad"
    },
    {
      "id": 47,
      "text": "actors? Okay, let me translate this into a modern nightmare because I think I get it. Go for it. It's like trying"
    },
    {
      "id": 48,
      "text": "to organize a dinner via a massive group text. Oh, that is a nightmare. Right. You need everyone to agree on a restaurant and"
    },
    {
      "id": 49,
      "text": "a time. But here's the catch: you know for an absolute fact that half the people in this group text are actively lying"
    },
    {
      "id": 50,
      "text": "or they are actively trying to sabotage the plans. Just chaotic evil group chat members. Exactly. Some are sending delayed"
    },
    {
      "id": 51,
      "text": "messages. Some are sending contradictory locations just to mess with you. How on earth do you still manage to reach a consensus"
    },
    {
      "id": 52,
      "text": "and all show up at the exact same pizzeria at 7pm? That is a perfect modern equivalent. It's a real problem. It"
    },
    {
      "id": 53,
      "text": "is. And what's fascinating here is that Satoshi didn't just"
    },
    {
      "id": 54,
      "text": "invent a currency. They solved this decades-old computer science problem by basically eliminating the need for trust."
    },
    {
      "id": 55,
      "text": "OK, so how did they actually solve it? Well, Satoshi solved it through a mechanism called proof-of-work, which is driven by participants we call"
    },
    {
      "id": 56,
      "text": "miners. OK, I need to stop you there because the word miner immediately makes people think of, like, pickaxes and gold dust. Right."
    },
    {
      "id": 57,
      "text": "Yeah, the imagery is a bit misleading. What are these miners actually doing? So, in this network, miners are"
    },
    {
      "id": 58,
      "text": "simply specialized computers. That's it. Just computers. Just computers. And they dedicate massive amounts of computing power to"
    },
    {
      "id": 59,
      "text": "perform incredibly complex repetitive computational tasks. Essentially, they are racing to solve."
    }
  ]
}
